; frame_pass_control — `controls` in a `(pass …)` prelude (plan §4.5).
;
; A `(pass …)`'s prelude owns @group(0), so an author-declared binding has
; nowhere to land and the pass has no host-settable parameter of its own.
; Declaring one control and naming `controls` in the WGSL closes that gap:
; the prelude declares `struct ControlInputs`, the hook creates one shared
; 16-byte buffer, and the first pass that names `controls` writes it once per
; frame. The struct's member order is the declaration order, so the shader
; reads the same value a guard would.
;
; `warp` bends the rings and drifts with pngine.time. The default 0.5 shows a
; gentle bend; a JS host moves it with setUniform(p, 'warp', 1.0), or the CLI
; with `--control warp=1`. Only a document that declares a control reserves
; the name, so a pass in a document with none may still use `controls` as its
; own variable (D5).
;
; What you see: warm concentric rings centred left of the canvas, bending
; more sharply as `warp` rises.

(control :name warp :default 0.5)

(pass-graph
  (pass :name main :code """
    @fragment fn fs(@builtin(position) pos: vec4f) -> @location(0) vec4f {
      let uv = pos.xy / vec2f(pngine.width, pngine.height);
      let p = uv - vec2f(0.4, 0.5);
      let r = length(p);
      let rings = 0.5 + 0.5 * sin(r * (20.0 + controls.warp * 60.0) - pngine.time * 2.0);
      let tint = mix(vec3f(0.9, 0.35, 0.1), vec3f(0.95, 0.85, 0.35), rings);
      return vec4f(tint * (0.35 + 0.65 * rings), 1.0);
    }
  """))
